A: I understand you want to add an observable statement to the contract test to assert the condition that the index where x is being inserted is less than or equal to the last index of x in the new array.
So you can write the following:
// an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ the index where x is being inserted is less than or equal to last index of x in new array
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX); 
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX); 
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);

However, note that the assertion will fail instantly if the condition is not satisfied. It won't halt the execution, but it will mark it as failed in the Pex test results.
Also, note that the Debug.Assert statement will only be observed if Pex is running with the "Show Observe Variables" options. If you cannot see the "$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", "$new_arrList_IndexOf_x" in the report, you may disable the Assert statements.
Note: Do not forget to import Debug namespace by adding 'using System.Diagnostics;' to your test code.
Hope it helps.
Feel free to get back if you have more questions or if this doesn't help at all.

A: Pex currently does not support observing assertions or debug.assert's in the Pex report, so your proposed solution will not work. It has a limitation that you have to be careful about while designing and testing contracts based on any conditionals/assertions in a method, since if you change these statements, the Pex framework will treat it as a new method and may generate different tests and therefore, different results as well. It is best to stick to simple boolean conditions and no complex expressions (like conditionals with multiple conditions or AND/OR operations) in the input parameters as Pex may generate different inputs for each condition and hence different sets of tests.
I suggest you to consider using NUnit or XUnit attribute [ExpectedException] for your contract based tests. If you are using Pex in combination with such a framework, you can then specify the expected exception types in your tests.
Also note that Pex is a white box testing tool and does a great job at identifying edge cases and complex scenarios in your code, but is not designed to catch assertion failures or contract testing scenarios that fail outside the scope of Pex tests.
Hope this helps and best of luck with your testing.

A: It seems like you are looking for a assertion within your test conditions. Unfortunately, Microsoft Pex does not support assertions. Pex is a white-box testing tool, used to explore the code paths in isolation from assertions, exception handling, and inputs that were not covered in previous tests (partial coverage).
So, assertions (or throw exceptions) that you have in the code are not going to be tested by Pex. If you want to validate some conditions at the result of your method execution, you'll have to check them by yourself or use another tool that allows this kind of operations.
One other suggestion is make use of Pex's coverage analysis tool, which can help you determine which methods are being tested by the Pex tool and which parts of your code are not being tested.
