A: I've implemented using Pex/PexLib in the following way:
    [PexMethod]
    public void MyFunc(List<int> list, int x)
    {
        Assume.LS(list.Count <= 10);

        var oldCount = list.Count;

        list.Add(x);

        var newCount = list.Count;

        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_count", oldCount);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_count", newCount);

        Assume.LS(newCount == oldCount + 1);//new list count should be old list count + 1 
    } 

Basically using the assumption attribute to say that the length of your list cannot exceed 10, as if was your list maximum limit. For checking the result of Add function, assume it will increase the list count by 1.
The reason for saying "Assume.LS(list.Count <= 10)" is because Pex/MSPex are not able to handle large lists efficiently due to various internal restrictions.
Please also note, you will need to write more complex method for testing other operations like Remove(), RemoveAt(), Insert() etc using same principle as above. It would be great if you create a generic method to handle these things for you which will take the operation as a parameter, but that goes beyond the scope of this post. 
I hope this helps.

A: If you want to test a method that adds an item to the List, you can use the following form:
[PexMethod]
public void AddToListTestMethod([PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList list, int item)
{
    int oldCount = list.Count;
    list.Add(item);
    int newCount = list.Count;
    Assert.AreEqual(oldCount + 1, newCount);
}

Pex will generate numerous test cases based on your assumptions and constraints. In this case, we assume the List is under test and add an item. We observe the old and new count of the List and verify that the new count is one more than the old count.
Please note that even though you have a specific implementation for ArrayList, if possible, it would be more idiomatic to test generic collections with NUnit.
Regarding the other methods like Remove(), RemoveAt(), Insert() etc., you would use a similar format to this, but provide them the correct arguments. Always ensure that you are testing the operation you are expecting, and that it is consistent with its contract. If the operation changes any part of the List's state, you would need to check that state has changed as expected.
You might find it useful--------------------        }
    }
}

